From: Loong <1031313955@qq.com>
Subject: [PATCH] net: airoha: wait longer for a TX descriptor to complete

airoha_eth_send() hands the descriptor to the QDMA by moving the CPU
index, then polls its DONE bit for 100 us and returns -EAGAIN if it is not
set -- leaving q->head where it was.  The next send then rewrites the same
descriptor and moves the CPU index to the same place again, while the QDMA
may still be reading the first one: a timeout does not drop one frame, it
mangles two.  The web recovery server sends TCP segments in bursts of up
to 16, which is where this would show.

Poll for up to 10 ms instead.  A descriptor that is done still returns on
the first look, so the normal path is unchanged.
---
diff --git a/drivers/net/airoha_eth.c b/drivers/net/airoha_eth.c
--- a/drivers/net/airoha_eth.c
+++ b/drivers/net/airoha_eth.c
@@ -1132,7 +1132,16 @@
 	airoha_qdma_rmw(qdma, REG_TX_CPU_IDX(qid), TX_RING_CPU_IDX_MASK,
 			FIELD_PREP(TX_RING_CPU_IDX_MASK, index));
 
-	for (i = 0; i < 100; i++) {
+	/*
+	 * The CPU index above has already handed the descriptor over, so
+	 * giving up on it early is worse than waiting: head stays put, the
+	 * next send rewrites a descriptor the QDMA may still be reading, and
+	 * two frames come out mangled.  Whether DONE means fetched or sent
+	 * is not documented; if sent, 100 us is less than one full frame
+	 * takes at 100 Mbit/s.  10 ms covers one at 10 Mbit/s with room to
+	 * spare, and a descriptor that is done still returns at once.
+	 */
+	for (i = 0; i < 10000; i++) {
 		dma_unmap_unaligned(virt_to_phys(desc), sizeof(*desc),
 				    DMA_FROM_DEVICE);
 		if (desc->ctrl & QDMA_DESC_DONE_MASK)
